"use strict";(self.webpackChunkspa_template=self.webpackChunkspa_template||[]).push([[659],{4659:s=>{s.exports=JSON.parse('{"created":"2018-01-28T00:00:00","modified":"2018-01-28T11:12:00","id":1037,"title":"Lodash, Webpack and Tree Shaking","article":"<p>If you are importing lodash functions into your project using a builder like Webpack, even if you are being explicit on your imports, you are bringing in the entire library not just the required functions. That is a big hit to take on your download size if you are only using one or two functions from Lodash.</p>\\n<p>Basically tree shaking isn&#39;t as simple as &#39;turning it on&#39; in Webpack for any library, not just lodash. The reason being is Webpack has to play it safe when it does tree shaking just in case there are any side effects to the code/functions it is unaware of (see <a href=\\"#Webpack%204\\">Webpack 4</a>).</p>\\n<p>So this article is not a criticism of lodash, its just when you might only be using it for one function it can just be a more extreme example.</p>\\n<h3 id=\\"lodash\\">Lodash</h3>\\n<p><a href=\\"https://github.com/lodash/lodash\\">Lodash</a> is a brilliant library that provides some great utility functions that are useful in many Javascript applications. I highly recommend you look it up.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"whats-the-problem\\">Whats the problem?</h2>\\n<p>Well if you install lodash via npm/yarn then import it into project you will bring in the entire library, regardless whether you set up Webpack to handle tree shaking correctly.</p>\\n<p>E.g.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> _ <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'lodash\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// or </span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { isFunction } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'lodash\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// or</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">const</span> _ =  <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">require</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'lodash\'</span>);</code></pre>\\n<p><em>All of the above will result in the full library being pulled into the bundle.</em></p>\\n<p>The reasons are that Webpack cant be sure of side effects of excluding code, so it plays it safe, also the version that npm/yarn pulls with <code>npm i --save lodash</code> is already converted to CommonJS modules and no longer in ES module format. Webpack finds it even harder to split out used/unused code as CommonJS modules arent as deterministic.</p>\\n<h3 id=\\"the-easiest-solution\\">The Easiest Solution</h3>\\n<p>Like with most libraries if you are a using a generic imports, rather than file explicit imports, then you will pull in the entire library as Webpack cant determine whether there are any side effects. So the easiest solution is just get all developers to use explicit imports.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// Incorrect - pulls in the full library as it cant be certain of side effects</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { isFunction } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'lodash\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// or </span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> _ <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'lodash\'</span>;\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// Correct - pulls in only what is asked for</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> isFunction <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'lodash/isFunction\'</span>;</code></pre>\\n<p><strong>NB</strong> This is in fact good practice (at time of writing see <a href=\\"#Webpack%204\\">Webpack 4</a> below) because it works with many libraries.</p>\\n<p>However as programmers we know this is a simple thing to forget, or a new dev comes onto the project not knowing, so we can use a babel plugin <a href=\\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/babel-plugin-lodash\\">babel-plugin-lodash</a> which simply converts generic imports into more specific imports to allow Webpack to tree shake.</p>\\n<p>E.g. babel-plugin-lodash turns this:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> _ <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'lodash\'</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { add } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'lodash\'</span>\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">const</span> addOne = add(<span class=\\"hljs-number\\">1</span>)\\n_.map([<span class=\\"hljs-number\\">1</span>, <span class=\\"hljs-number\\">2</span>, <span class=\\"hljs-number\\">3</span>], addOne)</code></pre>\\n<p>Into this:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> _add <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'lodash/add\'</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> _map <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'lodash/map\'</span>\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">const</span> addOne = _add(<span class=\\"hljs-number\\">1</span>)\\n_map([<span class=\\"hljs-number\\">1</span>, <span class=\\"hljs-number\\">2</span>, <span class=\\"hljs-number\\">3</span>], addOne)</code></pre>\\n<p>And voila we have tree shaken the modules :)</p>\\n<h3 id=\\"what-about-lodash-es\\">What about lodash-es?</h3>\\n<p>There is also an npm package called <code>lodash-es</code>, which unlike lodash exports modules in es module, rather than CommonJS modules format. </p>\\n<p>Although, that seems like it would get round the initial problem, currently it doesnt, and still requires people use explicit imports (at time of writing see <a href=\\"#Webpack%204\\">Webpack 4</a> below).</p>\\n<h3 id=\\"make-it-even-smaller---lodash-webpack-plugin\\">Make it even smaller - lodash-webpack-plugin</h3>\\n<p>In combination with using explicit imports (or <a href=\\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/babel-plugin-lodash\\">babel-plugin-lodash</a>), we can combine it with a Webpack plugin  <a href=\\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/lodash-webpack-plugin\\">lodash-webpack-plugin</a> that can make the build even smaller, even after tree shaking (albeit much less of gain, than using explicit imports).</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"webpack-4\\">Webpack 4</h2>\\n<p>As mentioned a couple of time above, the reason for tree shaking not working as expected is Webpack cant be sure that removing some code doesnt have side effects, say a function calls a global that is not easy for Webpack to find etc. This is made &#39;slightly worse&#39; by libraries being helpful and providing index files that export all code from other files, to make imports easier for the developer. So Webpack plays it safe.</p>\\n<p>Webpack 4 introduces a new flag <code>sideEffects</code> that a library developer (like lodash) can add to thier libraries packages.json file to tell any project that installs it that Webpack can be more aggresive with its tree shaking.</p>\\n<p>This is a great advancement, but we will still have to wait for libraries to start adding the flag to their to their package.json files and some projects wont be able to because they have side effects anyway. So a good idea to be selective going forward if possible.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"final-thought\\">Final thought</h2>\\n<p>If you are using are using npm to bring in a library where you only use one or two simple functions, you could just avoid importing the library altogether and write your own... we are programmers after all ;)</p>\\n","showInResults":true,"tags":["javascript","webpack"]}')}}]);